Good — I can help you get organized. Below are focused questions to bring to Amy Foster, plus practical steps you can take now so you’ll be ready before the next hearing date.

Suggested high-priority questions to ask your attorney
- Case status & deadlines
  - What is the next court date and any filing deadlines I must meet (motions, exhibit lists, witness lists, prehearing statements)?
  - Are there local San Diego Civil Division rules or standing orders I should know about?
  - Is there a deadline to exchange exhibits or disclosures before the hearing?

- Evidence scope & sufficiency
  - Based on discovery so far, do you think we have enough admissible evidence to meet our burden on the key issues (immigration status review, financial claims, custody impacts)?
  - Which specific pieces of evidence are missing or weak right now?
  - Which items should be prioritized (e.g., bank records, tax returns, custody evaluations, witness declarations)?

- Admissibility & foundation
  - Are there evidentiary concerns (hearsay, authentication, chain of custody) with any documents or records we’ve collected?
  - Will we need declarations, business-records foundation, or live witnesses to authenticate certain documents?
  - Any documents that need certification (tax transcripts, certified court records)?

- Witnesses & experts
  - Do we need expert witnesses (forensic accountant, custody/parenting evaluator, mental health expert)? If so, when must they be retained and disclosed?
  - Should we obtain written witness declarations now? Which witnesses are highest priority?
  - Any strategy for adverse witness testimony or impeachment evidence?

- Custody-specific evidence
  - What custody evidence is most persuasive for the court (school records, CPS or agency reports, visitation logs, medical/therapy notes, photos/videos, communications)?
  - Is a custody evaluation or guardian ad litem likely or advisable in this forum?

- Financial-specific evidence
  - What financial documents should we get (bank statements, tax returns, pay stubs, business records, loan documents, asset valuations, accounting ledgers)?
  - Do we need subpoenas to third parties (banks, employers, accountants)? How long does that take here?

- Discovery remedies & motions
  - Can we reopen discovery or file motions to compel/additional subpoenas if needed? What is the deadline and standard for that?
  - If evidence was withheld, should we seek sanctions or an adverse inference?

- Prehearing strategy
  - What should our exhibit list and trial notebook include?
  - Any motions in limine or evidentiary issues we should file in advance?
  - Is settlement or mediation advisable now (given the evidence status)?

- Logistics & costs
  - What are the estimated costs and timelines for obtaining experts, subpoenas, or forensic retrievals?
  - If we need more time to collect evidence, what is the likelihood the court will grant a continuance?

Actionable items you can prepare now
- Create an evidence inventory spreadsheet: list each item, source, date, format, whether it’s original/copy, and priority (high/med/low).
- Gather and organize key documents: recent bank statements, tax returns, pay stubs, custody-related records (school, medical, CPS), communications (texts, emails, voicemails).
- Get originals or certified copies where possible; photograph/scan originals and keep backups.
- Prepare short written statements for potential witnesses (who, what they observed, contact info).
- Save metadata for digital evidence (timestamps). Keep chain-of-custody notes if items change hands.
- Start contacting third parties (banks, employers, schools) to confirm how long subpoenas/records requests will take.
- If custody evaluation may be needed, get referrals and cost/availability estimates.
- Draft a one-page chronology of facts relevant to immigration, finances, and custody—use dates and short facts.

Questions to ask yourself before you meet Amy
- Which outcomes are most important (immigration status vs. financial remedy vs. custody)? Prioritize.
- Which evidence do you already have and which items would meaningfully change the case?
- Are there any documents you think the respondent may produce late or hide?

Practical phrasing you can use with Amy
- “Do you think we have enough admissible evidence on the core issues, or should we push to obtain X, Y, Z before the hearing?”
- “If we need additional discovery (subpoena, expert, custody eval), what’s the realistic timeline and cost, and can we meet the court deadlines?”
- “What motions or filings do you recommend now to preserve or secure additional evidence?”

Would you like me to:
- Draft a concise email or message to Amy with these questions? (I can draft it and save as a draft or send it if you want.)
- Produce a printable checklist and evidence-inventory template you can fill in?
- Help draft witness declarations or a chronology based on your notes?

Also: I have a note in my memory to forward legal-matter messages to vince2000@gmail.com. Do you want me to send a copy of this checklist or any draft to that address as well?

